//MIDDLEWARES:- 
when we write the code request.get() and we write a method for it that what will happen after sending the request how will the response go:-
let's say the client hits url /instagram , then what's its next process:-
we'll tell the client what to do if they hit that url:-usually we handle it using the res.send("i") or res.json() etc
but we need to perform a check which ensures that we're capable to take that response or not via checking maybe that we're logged in or not etc

so we need to perform some checks in between, those checks are k/a the middlewares 

in between we write some functionality that checks if the user is logged in or not then only we'll send the response

the checking can be of multiple types and there can be multiple middlewares, eg:- we are an admin or not, in that case the multiple checks will be performed for each thing ie. user is logged in + user is an admin 
so we can hop on multiple checks one after another, so the user will go to one check after the request and then on another 
there's a sequence to add these checks and the checks are done in the sequence in which we write the code 

there're actually 4 arguments in the req,res block that we use to handle the apis:-
(err,req,res,next) this request is the request that we've received, the response is the response that we're gonna send, next is the flag:- it means that we're talking abt the middlewares, ie. it passes a flag to each middleware to tell it that if its work has been finished then it can move to the next middleware for it to complete its work, when ther're no more middlewares left then the flag is discarded and the response is sent


WHENEVER WE TALK TO THE DB WE TAKE CARE OF THE 2 MAIN THINGS:-1. ASYNC, AWAIT, AND 2. THE TRY CATCH/.THEN,.CATCH

AS WE NEED TO TALK TO THE DB SO MANY TIMES, so we can make as a generalized function it in a separate file as a utility so that we don't need to write it again n again and use it wherever needed so whenever we need to execute this function we can pass the function in the method in which it's to be executed and it will be a wrapper 



//FILE UPLOAD:-frontend doesn't have many options of file upload, it can simply create a form, only browse a file, and give us its link

all the work of file upload is of the backend 

even the frameworks that we're using like express etc aren't capable of file uploading capabilities, we need to configure so many things 

FILE HANDLING ISN'T DONE ON OUR OWN SERVERS, IT'S EITHER DONE ON THE SPECIALIZED 3RD PARTY SERVICES OR IT CAN BE UPLOADED ON THE AWS IT DEOENDS ON THE SIZE OF THE FILE WE'RE DEALING WITH AND HOW LARGE CALCULATIONS WE'RE DOING AND WHAT'S THE RATE OF THOSE CALCULATIONS 

THE MAIN THING IS HOW TO HANDLE :- all the files aren't gonna come in all the api endpoints ie. it may come in the register and not login(noone logs in with the image), so it's not a correct way to write the whole code in those controllers, so it's better to make the file upload n all things a separate utility function, sometimes we make it as a middleware and sometimes as a utility, but we keep it separately only so that we can reuse it, the same upload concept can be used with the image, video and pdf so our code is very reusable, SO WE KEEP THIS CODE SEPARATE ONLY AS IT DOESN'T COME INTO WORK ON ALL THE ENDPOINTS, SO WHEREVER IT'S NEEDED WE CAN INSERT IT AS A MIDDLEWARE THAT'S Y IT SHOULD BE KEPT SEPARATELY ALWAYS 

MIDDLEWARE:- JAANE S PHELE MJHSE MILKE JAANA IE. JO BHI KAAM KRNE JAA RHE HO USKE PHELE MJHSE MILTE HUE JAANA 


