HTTP vs HTTPS:- technology, communication and rules don't hv any major difference there's only a little difference in the protocol ie. the difference is that the data in http goes in the clear text only ie. if we send abc then abc only goes to the server, but in https, the data that we send is encrypted and is sent by performing many operations 
https:- one more layer comes in between that does the work of adding the keys/performing xor operations with our data so that it becomes unreadeable normally and becomes readable only at the server or whatever comes from the server, it becomes readable at the client that's y it's k/a https
HTTP:-hyper text transfer protocol

the text is in many formats and there's the data in many forms, so the thing that's to be seen is that how do we need to transfer the text 

//the main whole point is about how can we transfer the text or anything :- there're many variables and many options and we wanna transfer in the optimised manner so we don't wanna use any ds as such that's very expensive so we need the dsa knowledge
As the computers are the 2 OS talking to each other on a network, so the networking comes into play and the OS will also store that data so the knowledge of OS also comes into play

URL:-UNIFORM RESOURCE LOCATOR:- where's its location 
URI:-UNIFORM RESOURCE IDENTIFIER
URN:-UNIFORM RESOURCE NAME

there's not much difference in all the 3, the main thing is that we need the address in order to talk to it, so we need its location and we're not sure that the url of the location will be http only, just like in case of mongodb we had the protocols like srn,srv instead of http 
everything has a protocol to communicate and http is one of them
and there're different names of all of them so we say the common technical name of them to be the URI/URL ie. we need the name of that resource that how's being identified uniquely so in big companies people use URI/URN instrad of using address/URL 



HTTP HEADERS:-
when we send the http request then we also need to add some additional information ie. the info that we need to include while sending the file anywhere for eg:- when we're sending the file, then we need to send some data along with the file, ie. when the file has been created, what's the name of the file, when was it modified so there's some metadata that comes along with the file, and the HTTP HEADERS ARE ALSO THE SAME ie. it's the data abt the data ie. the metadata and this metadata is in the form of the key value pairs eg:- name:hitesh 
these key values in the http headers are kept so much opened ie. eg:- some headers r already defined ie. what'll be the output on getting a particular input, but we can also make our own headers and we get the headers both in request and response 
when we send the request then as per the location and the method of the request sent, if we've used any tool like postman/thunderclient or something or sent the information programatically in order to send the request or if we've used any tool to send it, so the headers are sent as per that and if we've sent the request from the browser, then some different headers are sent and it also depends upon the response ie. what sort of the response the server is sending us, ie. okay or 404-not found 
so there're the headers both in the response as well as the request and the headers are the metadata 

THE WORKS OF HEADERS:- used for 
CACHING:- to see that the request had come recently only so we can pick up the data/some values from the cache database if we get them then
they're also used for authentication:-there're many headers of AUTHENTICATION as well ie. if there're bearer tokens/session values/session cookies/refresh tokens or anything so they're also used for the authentication works
STATE MANAGEMENT:-what's the state of the user ie. if it's a guest user or a logged in user if the user has kept anything in the cart or not, so we also see the state of the user

previously, while sending the headers, we were also required to send the prefix 'x' ie. if we're sending the name then we need to send the name in the format:- x-name 

CATEGORIES OF HEADERS:- 
1 REQUEST HEADERS:-the data coming from the client is k/a the request headers 
2 RESPONSE HEADERS:-whatever data is coming from the servers, it's k/a the response headers 
3 REPRESENTATION HEADERS:- tell in which representation our data is, ie. the data is in which encoding or in which compression we'll handle so many cases in which we'll get the data in the form of zip or others so we need to extract it first and then we represent it, also it's used in applications using graph charts, and also in some applications like zerodha, razorpay there we send the data in the compressed form and we use graph charts in it as the network has a limit that we can send only a particular amount of data only via the network otherwise there're lags so in that case we use the representation headers to see what're the encodings/compressions of the data 
4 payload headers:- payload is a fancy name for data, whatever data we need to send, ie. if we normally wanna send the id or the email of someone then it's all the payload

MOST COMMON HEADERS:-
1 Accept headers/metadata:- tells what kind of metadata/data are we gonna accept, it's mostly on the servers (application/json,text/html)
2 User-agent:- tells from which appplication the request has come ie. from postman, or the browser has sent the request, so which browser it was and which engine was it supporting, was it the browser of geco engine or saffari and from which os
so while visiting some webistes, they give a popup to use their app, all that's taken from the user agent metadata only, ie. if the request is coming from the mobile browser and the app of the website also exists then they can send the popup to download their app
3 authorization:- it's the most common metadata in the frontend, ie. Bearer ___________, this blank contains the long line of the code, these r just the jwt bearer tokens that we send 
4 content-type:- it tells what all r we sending ie. the images/pdfs/ or whatever
5 cookies:-it contains the key val pairs that hv the info abt how mcuh time the cookies will exist and what's the unique code and till what time we'll login the user
6 cache control:- contains till when should we expire if the data wants to reamin in the network 

SOME OTHER HEADERS THAT MAY BE PRESENT IN PRODUCTION GRADE APPS:-
CORS HEADERS :- they don't do anything by themselves, it's just an origin policy by the company that from where r we allowing the origin from where ie. what all requests r we allowing to our application/ from where  :-
1 access-control-allow-origin
2 access-control-allow-credentials
3 access-control-allow-method:- eg:- allow GET and not POST

SECURITY HEADERS:-
1 cross-origin-embedder-policy
2 cross-origin-opener-policy
3 content-security-policy
4 X-XCS-protection

all these headers have to be written by us that what all will happen from all the code/the metadata that we'll be writing, it's not something that's happening directly ie. it's not all written automacially, we've to write the code for each n everything


HTTP METHODS:-tells what set of operations r being performed in order to interact with the server

1 GET:- retrive and resource (give all the users/some users with a specific email)
2 HEAD:- give only the headers as a response ie. there's no body msg in this case eg:- many times we wanna see the user agent/cache control in that case we create the endpoints that contain only headers and no body
3 OPTIONS:-we can ask the server what all otpions r available at the specific url:- is GET available at /users, is POST available at /users etc
4 TRACE:- it only sends the loopback ie. gives just the response to whatever request we've sent, used only for debugging 
many times our resource is behind many proxies, so at which proxies the request is coming and the request is going by going via which proxies if we wanna do debugging , or if the response is taking a longer time to come/there's hopping then related to all those concepts then we use trace
5 DELETE:- removes the resource
6 PUT:- replaces a resource ie. if any of our resource is kept via the email id and we want the complete object to get replaced 
7 POST:-interacts with the resource (mostly adds the value ie. adding a new user/product/category)
8 PATCH:-when we wanna change only a specific part of the resource ie. we wanna edit

all of these methods hv to be made in our controllers, it's nothing automatic


HTTP STATUS CODE:-
1xx (means the range starting from 1 after which we can use only 2 more digits):- used to pass only some information to the user 
2xx (range starting from 2 ie. 201,200,202 etc):- used to tell the user that whatever operation they wanted to perform has been successfully performed + whatever data they'd sent has been successfully received by the user
3xx (301,300,302 etc):- used for redirection ie. the resource that we wanted to view/method that we're trying to access has been moved somewhere else/removed either temporarily/permanentally
4xx (400,401,402 etc):-used for client error eg:- the client tried to login/perform some operation without sending the token/entered the wrong password. ie/ the information hasn't been rceived correctly from the client side
5xx (500,501,502 etc):- used for server error eg:- the client sent the image properly and we tried to upload it by calling the api and due to the network break/ network traffic/congestion and due to come reason the image couldn't upload, so in that case we say that the error has come from the server side and so we should retry to do that operation 

100:- continue, 102:- processing, 200:-ok, 201:-resource created 

for testing the  api/doing the api testing:- use POSTMAN



//ACCESS AND REFRESH TOKENS:-
//ACCESS TOKEN:- jb tk ye h tb tk koi bhi feature jha p authentication ki requirement h vha p uss resource ko access kr skte h
for eg:- not evveryone will be allowed to upload the files but if we're authenticated/logged in then we can upload them
but uf our login session is expired after 15mins due to security reasons then we'll hv to enter the pass after 15mins to login again

REFRESH TOKEN:-we save it in the db as well and give it to the user also
so, we validate the user using the access token only but if the user has the refresh token then we make the user to hit an endpoint so that by hitting the endpoint, if the refresh token with the user matches with that of the db then they will get a new access token and won't hv to enter the pass again and can directly login 



ALL ABOUT REFRESH TOKEN AND ACCESS TOKEN:-
they only hv the work that the user doesn't hv to enter the email and  password again n again

ACCESS TOKEN:-IT'S SHORT LIVED, for eg:- one day only, after one day, the user will hv to enter the id password nd login again in order to refresh the token

SO THERE'RE 2 TYPES OF TOKENS:- THE ACCESS TOKEN THAT'S SHORT LIVED AND NOT SAVED ANYWHERE AND IS GIVEN TO THE USER SO THAT THE USER CAN WORK CONSTANTLY 
REFRESH TOKEN:-THE LONG LIVED TOKEN THAT'S STORED IN THE DB AND WHEN THE TIMER OF THE ACCESS TOKEN GETS EXPIRED, THE USER GETS TO KNOW THAT HIS/HER SESSION HAS EXPIRED SO THEY CAN EXTEND THEIR SESSION AND WHILE DOING SO THEY SEND A REFRESH TOKEN TO THE DB, WHICH IS MATCHED WITH THE REFRESH TOKEN STORED IN THE DB AND IF THE TOKEN MATCHES THEN THE SESSION GETS EXTENDED AND ALSO THE USERS GET BOTH OF THEIR ACCESS AND REFRESH TOKEN REFRESHED VIA HITTING THIS ENDPOINT, ELSE THE USER NEEDS TO REGISTER 


